      ],
      "specification": {
        "text": "Given train-only embeddings x_i in R^d, compute mu = mean_i(x_i), C = (1/N) sum_i (x_i-mu)(x_i-mu)^T, and eigendecomposition C=U diag(lambda) U^T. Set A=U diag((lambda+alpha*mean(lambda))^(-1/2)) U^T, with fixed alpha=0.05. Train the unchanged RQ-VAE on z_i=A(x_i-mu) with its existing reconstruction, commitment, and codebook losses; at export, encode each item's same z_i and write its ordinary single code tuple. Do not fit A using validation/test items or labels."
      },
      "title": "Whiten item semantics before residual quantization"
    },
    {
      "also_proposed_by": [
        "ideation_2"
      ],
      "cost_estimate": "The per-step change is K scalar divisions/multiplications on existing tensors and no additional pairwise distances or optimizer state: O(batch*d*K) elementwise work, expected well below 1% of baseline RQ-VAE step time. Scale estimation is one train-item pass after warm-up. At 24556 items, 24 steps/epoch, and 3000 epochs this remains feasible on one GPU.",
      "duplicate_count": 1,
      "falsifiable_hypothesis": "The fixed scales make later-stage code usage and per-stage residual-energy reduction less degenerate, and this translates into higher validation and test next-item R@10 than the untempered model (targeting improvement over test_R@10 0.06335). If only code utilization changes without a repeatable ranking gain, discard the idea.",
      "fingerprint": "c86b495565b2e78f",
      "idea_id": "residual_norm_tempering",
      "key_change": "Normalize the residual entering each later stage by a fixed stage-specific scalar, while accumulating the correspondingly rescaled quantized contribution, so all stages operate on comparable residual scales without adding trainable parameters.",
      "motivation": "In residual quantization, early-stage residuals can be much larger than later residuals, so reconstruction-driven training may over-optimize coarse semantic directions while fine code digits encode little useful distinction. This can make two items with different fine-grained needs share overly similar prefixes or leave later digits underused.",
      "novelty": "This changes the scale of the residual presented to each successive quantizer using a deterministic scalar schedule, rather than changing geometry, branching, assignment balancing, or adding a behavior-derived objective. It keeps the same codebooks, number of stages, and global item-to-tuple assignment.",
      "origin_agent": "ideation_2",
      "risks": [
        "Stage scales estimated from an early checkpoint may be stale; scaling may interact badly with the commitment/codebook loss magnitudes; and normalizing a stage could encourage fine digits to encode noise. Use a fixed scale protocol decided before the run, log per-stage residual and usage statistics, and evaluate with identical Stage 3 settings."
      ],
      "specification": {
        "text": "For encoder output h_i and K residual codebooks, initialize r_i^(0)=h_i. For stage l, compute s_l = sqrt(mean_train(||r_i^(l)||_2^2)/d) once from a short baseline warm-up checkpoint (stop-gradient, train items only), then quantize u_i^(l)=r_i^(l)/max(s_l,1e-6) with the existing nearest-code rule. Set q_i^(l)=s_l * e_{a_i^l}^{(l)}, r_i^(l+1)=r_i^(l)-q_i^(l), and reconstruct h-hat_i=sum_l q_i^(l). Freeze s_l for the subsequent training run; use the same scales at export. Compare against an identical baseline warm-up and exclude held-out items from scale estimation."
      },
      "title": "Temper residual magnitudes across quantization stages"
    },
    {
      "also_proposed_by": [
        "ideation_3"
      ],
      "cost_estimate": "One streaming pass over training sequences and O(number of items) accumulators; negligible relative to baseline and no added per-step training work. At 24 steps per epoch and 3000 epochs, incremental cost is exactly 0 tensor operations per optimization step and under one additional data pass before training; fits easily on one GPU.",
      "duplicate_count": 1,
      "falsifiable_hypothesis": "The appended role token improves test_R@10 over 0.06335, with the largest gains for items whose position profiles differ despite similar baseline content codes. Measure overall Recall@10 and stratified performance by item occurrence count and position-bin separation; reject if role assignment does not improve held-out next-item accuracy or gains are confined to frequent items.",
      "fingerprint": "a0b39528d3a1fe53",
      "idea_id": "train_position_role_sid",
      "key_change": "Estimate a smoothed position profile for each item using training histories only, quantize its mean relative position into a small number of bins, and append that bin as one global SID token. No per-user state or inference-time lookup is required.",
      "motivation": "Items with similar content can play different roles in user journeys: some tend to appear as early exploration, while others are commonly revisited or occur late in a history. Content-derived IDs can merge such items even though their next-item implications differ.",
      "novelty": "Unlike transition graphs, demand/repeat signatures, or uncertainty-based stability, this describes an item's marginal position within observed training histories and adds a tiny categorical role token while preserving its existing content SID.",
      "origin_agent": "ideation_3",
      "risks": [
        "Position can reflect dataset truncation or sequence-construction conventions rather than a stable user journey role.",
        "The added token may be redundant with the generator's history context, or may fragment useful content prefixes and reduce generalization for rare items.",
        "Position marginals discard which preceding context caused the role, so the bins may be too coarse to produce predictive distinctions."
      ],
      "specification": {
        "text": "For each training sequence s=(i_1,...,i_L), ignore sequences with L<2 and define x_{s,t}=(t-1)/(L-1). For item i accumulate n_i and m_i=sum x_{s,t} over all occurrences. Let mu_i=(m_i+alpha*mu_0)/(n_i+alpha), where mu_0 is the global mean x over all counted positions and alpha=5. On training items with n_i>0, compute quartile cut points of mu_i; assign role r_i in {0,1,2,3} by these cuts, with n_i=0 assigned to the global-median bin. Export SID(i)=baseline_RQ_SID(i) concatenated with r_i (a single token from a four-token vocabulary); do not alter existing token IDs. Compute all statistics and cut points from training histories only, freeze them, and evaluate with unchanged Stage 3 settings."
      },
      "title": "Encode an item's train-only sequence-position role"
    }
  ],
